# jarvis-engine: the root helper of the server's BASE LAYER (decisions of 10.10.2026: the server = base layer + engines).
#
# The base layer stays on every server and is never removed: the puller (updates), this helper (a closed list of
# root actions), the base pulse (alive + engines + usage of the day, no conversation content), the firewall, the
# VS Code tunnel. Engines come and go on the owner's buttons: Jarvis (Claude Code + our Telegram bot) and the native
# OpenClaw (its own Telegram bot, its own interface). One bot token = one listening program, so each engine has its
# own bot and add-openclaw refuses a token of a Jarvis bot (TOKEN_IN_USE).
#
# LOCAL ONLY. A button in the owner's bot writes ONE JSON request into the bot's own request directory:
#     Jarvis bot      /home/client/.msb/engine/request      (~/.msb is in the bot's ReadWritePaths)
#     OpenClaw plugin /home/openclaw/.jarvis-engine/request
# jarvis-engine.path (root) starts `jarvis-engine run`, which opens the file through an O_NOFOLLOW descriptor chain,
# DELETES it first, then judges the descriptor (regular, owner = the expected user, single link, <= 4096 bytes), parses
# strict JSON (duplicate keys refused), checks every value by regex against the closed list below, and acts. Our API is
# not in this path: the bot token and a model key go from the owner's bot to this server and nowhere else. Nothing
# from a request is executed as shell: the action picks one of our embedded scripts; secrets reach them only as a
# root-only file of the run or on stdin, never as an argument, never in a log line or in state.json.
#
# The server's state AND the result of every request go to /run/jarvis-engine/state.json (0644, rebuilt by the tick
# 1 min after boot and after every request; no token, key, owner id or bot username in it). The bot polls it for the
# id it wrote (`last`, `results.<source>`) and shows only the actions listed in `actions`.
# Contract for the bots: src/engine-helper/CONTRACT.md.
#
# Destructive actions never erase at once (the agent lives under the bot's own user and can be talked into writing a
# request): remove-* moves what the owner keeps into ~/.msb/archive/<engine>-<stamp>/ and the rest of the owner's data
# into ~/.msb/archive/.trash/<engine>-<stamp>/, erased by the tick no earlier than TRASH_TTL after the stamp; `restore`
# brings the trash back into the archive until then. Keys, tokens and config of a removed OpenClaw wait the same TTL in
# a root-only trash (never in a user's HOME). Removing or switching off the LAST working engine is refused: the owner
# always keeps one bot to press buttons in. Removing Jarvis stops its bot LAST: the helper announces the stop
# (phase jarvis_stop_pending), the bot says goodbye and writes ~/.msb/engine/stop-ack, then (or after 60 s) the helper
# disables and masks the unit, so neither the puller nor bot-ctl.sh nor a reboot brings it back. Removing is offered
# only with a way back (state.json `way_back`: OpenClaw installed, switched on, its /server plugin wired), else
# NO_WAY_BACK.
# The engines answer one at a time (Tom 10.10: «переключение, а не одновременно»): switching Jarvis off is SLEEP, per
# bot, not a mask. disable-jarvis puts ~/.msb/engine/sleep.<bot id> (written by client itself); the bot's process
# stays up (its heartbeat and our commands go on), it starts no agent turn and answers every message with «сейчас
# работает OpenClaw» and a button that wakes it (the bot deletes its own mark). enable-jarvis wakes it from here.
#
# Subcommands: run | setup | tick | status | pulse | snapshot | --version
#   run     the requests (jarvis-engine.service, started by jarvis-engine.path).
#   setup   dirs, the helper's own units, the static update path where absent, the OpenClaw /server plugin files
#           (changed files flag plugin.stale for the tick), the backup's snapshot drop-in; idempotent; setup.sh runs it
#           at install, the updater after each update.
#   tick    1 min after boot, then every 15 min (jarvis-engine-tick.timer): trash past its TTL erased, a failed OpenClaw
#           gateway revived (unless the owner switched it off), the puller guarded (an enabled timer kept running, a
#           failed tick unit reset, a damaged puller.cjs restored from the root copy taken at install), flagged plugin
#           files wired and reloaded in an installed OpenClaw, OpenClaw moved up to the version pinned here (S5: unless
#           the owner switched updates off; one try a day, never while somebody talks to it), an OpenClaw of an older
#           helper brought to upstream's defaults once (0.5.6, the owner's own settings kept), the bot's Russian menu kept
#           in step with OpenClaw's default one (0.5.6), state.json rebuilt.
#   status  prints state.json (root: rebuilt first).
#   pulse   NOT root (msb-base-pulse.service, User=client): POST /api/heartbeat/base with the box's SYNC_URL/SYNC_KEY.
#   snapshot  root, before the nightly backup (msb-backup.service ExecStartPre, our drop-in): a copy of the native
#           OpenClaw without model keys and sign-ins into ~/.msb/archive/openclaw-snapshot/ + ~/.msb/archive/engines.json
#           (the backup archives /home/client only). The tick keeps the drop-in; see «OpenClaw snapshot» below.
# Updates: the puller delivers a new build to ~/.msb/engine/jarvis-engine.next (src/fleet/managed-paths.ts); the
# separate updater (/usr/local/lib/jarvis-engine/update, never replaced by a helper update) installs it.
